170 families in land dispute with 14 other families in Thmar Puok district have not yet been resolved
(Banteay Meanchey): 170 families involved in a long-running land dispute with 14 other families in the Phnom Preah area in Thmor Puok district, Banteay Meanchey province, requested the relevant authorities to resolve and find justice for them after Growing potatoes or corn, the other group removed all the machinery.
Mr. Sam Vutha, representing 170 families, said that they have been suffering from land disputes since 2000. He said that in the past, they had encroached on Phnom Preah forest land in Thmar Puok district to take ownership of it.
Later, the government, through youth volunteers, measured and gave land titles to the people, and suddenly they measured the land registration for only 14 families.
Due to this, they also objected to the issuance of 293.14 hectares of disputed land titles. However, they still enjoy it every year, but for the last three years, another group named Sam Mab, who gave them money to run the land titles, has not given them any more. Both went to work for the company to use machinery to clear their crops.
The people would like to ask the authorities to facilitate them to continue to grow crops. They have filed a lawsuit against Sam Mab, but so far the court has not acted.
Regarding this case, Mr. Em Sokha, the governor of Thmar Puok district, said that the land dispute occurred before he became the district governor and this matter has reached the national level and the court, which is beyond his jurisdiction.
