Southeast Asia's second-largest economy has been hit by a slowdown led by China, its main trading partner, and a drop in investor confidence due to the protracted post-election political crisis. .
Thailand's gross domestic product rose 1.8 percent in April-June from a year earlier, Reuters reported, citing data released by the National Economic and Social Development Board (NESDC). Last year, it was lower than economists' estimates in a Reuters poll of 3.1 percent.
In the first quarter, GDP rose 2.6 percent from a year earlier, down from 2.7 percent a year earlier.
The second quarter was the worst hit for Thailand as exports fell 5.7 percent from a year earlier, sending output down 3.3 percent. The Thai government also fell 4.3 percent. All of this put further pressure on fixed asset investment, which fell 2.8 percent in the quarter.
The slump in global demand has prompted the Thai government to cut its growth forecast for 2023 to between 2.5 percent and 3 percent, from an increase of 2.7 percent to 3.7 percent. That the central bank may not be in a hurry to raise interest rates again. According to Reuters.
