(KPL) On the morning of August 21, 2023, in Samarang, Republic of Indonesia, Dr. Kao Kim Hourn, Secretary-General of ASEAN, attended the 12th ASEAN Economic Ministers' Meeting with Russia. The meeting welcomed the progress of the implementation of the ASEAN-Russia Trade and Investment Work Program 2022-2023.
The ASEAN Secretary-General shared his views on possible joint cooperation in strategic areas such as trade, digital economy, sustainable economic development and climate change, as well as innovative economies to strengthen economic ties. Between ASEAN and Russia more.
Afterwards, Dr. Kao Kim Hourn attended the ASEAN Economic Ministers' Consultation with the US Trade Representative. On that occasion, the ASEAN Secretary-General shared his views on ways to further strengthen economic ties between ASEAN and the United States.
The ASEAN Secretary-General met with US Trade Representative Katherine Tai. Both sides welcomed the progress of ASEAN-US cooperation and agreed to work closely together to strengthen ASEAN-US relations.
Later, the ASEAN Secretary-General was invited to attend the 20th ASEAN Economic Ministers' Consultation with India. The meeting encouraged officials to strengthen efforts to review the ASEAN-India Commodity Trade Agreement (AITIGA). The meeting also included a dialogue with the ASEAN-India Business Council.
Dr. Kao Kim Hourn also participated in the consultation between the ASEAN Economic Ministers and the Chinese Ministry of Commerce. The meeting encouraged officials to strengthen negotiations to conclude negotiations on the ASEAN-China Free Trade Agreement as soon as possible within the agreed time.
The ASEAN Secretary-General was invited to attend the 11th East Asia Summit Economic Ministers' Meeting. The meeting highlighted the importance of strengthening practical cooperation among the countries participating in the East Asia Summit to strengthen economic resilience in the region.
Sydney: Australia's largest warship participates in joint exercises with the Philippines and the United States in the disputed South China Sea on Monday (August 21) as they seek to boost defense ties. In the face of China's growing military presence. This is according to the release by the APF news agency on August 21, 2023.
More than 2,000 troops from Australia and the Philippines are taking part in air, sea and land exercises from August 14 to 31. About 150 US Marines are also participating.
The United States, Japan and Australia also plan to hold joint naval exercises outside the Philippines this week. China has deployed hundreds of coastguards as well as navies and other vessels to patrol the reefs in the disputed waters, which it claims are almost entirely its own.
