Senior officials call on people to change the name of epilepsy to "epilepsy" to eliminate discrimination
(Phnom Penh): Professor Hun Chamroeun, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Health, called on people to change the name of epilepsy to "epilepsy" in order to jointly eliminate discrimination and its proper use in accordance with global standards.
His remarks were made at the 4th Annual Medical Conference of the Cambodian Neurological Association and the Cambodian Association Against Epilepsy under the theme "Epilepsy" on August 19, 2023.
Professor Hun Chamroeun said that epilepsy, which often manifests as recurrent seizures, used to be called epilepsy. This word sounds easy to understand but causes problems.
In addition, the word insanity causes the patient as well as the family to feel ashamed, and in addition, discrimination makes the patient suffer twice and does not dare to seek the right treatment. At a time when medicine in Cambodia is not yet advanced, people often associate this disease with superstition, seek religious healing, there are rituals, etc. And to this day, this practice is still practiced.
"Therefore, we call on the people to change the name of this epilepsy to epilepsy in order to eliminate discrimination and come together to help patients get the right medical treatment, because this disease can be cured. "No race and can get married, start a family and run a business like you." This is under the emphasis of Professor Hun Chamroeun.
"Apart from renaming the disease appropriately and universally, it is very important to increase the capacity for local treatment," he said. The association as well as the Ministry of Health is continuing to strengthen and expand the capacity to treat epilepsy to the health center level, both human resources as well as regular supply of medicine.
On that occasion, the professor also encouraged the two associations to continue to participate in raising awareness among the general population. In the past, the Cambodian Association of Neurologists and the Cambodian Association against Epilepsy have contributed a lot to the work of promoting well-being related to the nervous system, and especially the epilepsy.
These include scientific studies in Prey Veng and later in Tbong Khmum, which have published international research results for the benefit of both Cambodians and people around the world. In addition to research, both associations are also involved in raising awareness and therapeutic capacity among doctors and medical students.
The Cambodian Association Against Epilepsy was established in 2006 and is a member of the International Federation against Epidemiology (ILAE) with the overall responsibility of improving the maintenance, treatment and protection of physical health. Hearts and social bonds to epilepsy patients. This is according to Ms. Ros Sina, President of the Anti-Epilepsy Association.
