The decision to introduce a health care system for those working in the informal economy of tourism reflects the careful consideration of the well-being of the Cambodian people, based on the principle of "not abandoning any citizen."
He said that currently about 65% of Cambodians spend their own money on treatment, which is still high.
He said that the implementation of health care programs for individuals working in the field of entertainment services will be divided into two, in which for those who have a decent standard of living, get a CES card, which is used to treat patients for free, but for Individuals living in poverty The government will provide equity or poor cards that they may receive in addition to free medical treatment.
Dr. John Narith appealed to those who are working in the field of entertainment services, especially women who provide private entertainment services in other locations, to register to receive this certificate for use in health care free. Charge.
According to preliminary figures released by the National Council for Social Protection, there are currently more than 30,000 entertainment workers nationwide.
Im Nith, 56, currently working in a massage parlor in Daun Penh district, expressed her pleasure and gratitude to the Royal Government for launching a health care program for people working in the entertainment industry. A large part of the cost burden for workers in the sector.
No different 42-year-old woman who applied for a scholarship said that this card is very useful for those who work in the field of entertainment services, because every time you go for medical treatment, it costs a lot of money. A lot.
Dr. Ros Silavath, Deputy Director of the National AIDS Authority, said that the health care program for individuals working in the entertainment sector is a national policy that is important and targeted at the people in the sector because they A group of people who are at risk of falling into poverty due to various diseases.
He added that people in this sector are more susceptible to various infectious diseases, especially HIV, which makes them more susceptible to opportunistic diseases such as tuberculosis, pneumonia, meningitis and chronic diarrhea. When they face these diseases, they will no longer be able to go out to work and earn a lot of money on care and treatment.
He said that the social protection program has really helped to alleviate a large part of their lives, which makes them able to access better health care services. In particular, through this social protection policy will help to achieve the reduction of HIV transmission in Cambodia.
Through a recent inter-ministerial proclamation, the Royal Government requires employers or business owners in the tourism sector of the informal economy to prepare a list of individuals working in the tourism business of the informal economy in the enterprise or institution. And shall be sent to the Capital-Province or Municipality / District / Khan Administration for verification before submitting to the National Social Security Fund (NSSF) for registration.
In order to receive health services, individuals working in the tourism sector of the informal economy must use public health facilities, following the system of providing services from primary to lower health facilities to higher health facilities, except in emergencies.
