(Yangon): Myanmar expects its rice exports to increase in the coming months as India's export ban and rising Thai and Vietnamese rice prices force buyers to look for other sources. .
Bloomberg News quoted Ye Min Aung, president of the Myanmar Rice Federation, as saying in an interview last week that tightening global rice supplies should help revive Myanmar's rice exports. It fell 56 percent in the first four months of the fiscal year, bringing the Southeast Asian country closer to its annual target of $ 1 billion in rice exports.
Myanmar is a country facing food insecurity, a situation worsened by political instability since the military coup in 2021, and its export policy is designed to protect domestic supply.
Myanmar earned more than $ 800 million a year from rice sales for the past two fiscal years, with the country's biggest buyers being China, the Philippines and Belgium, according to the Myanmar Rice Federation. The US Department of Agriculture ranked Myanmar as the world's sixth largest exporter last year. According to Bloomberg News.
