This is the first confirmation of the rise in rice prices following a surprise ban by India that has forced importers to pay extra for grain, the world's most widely used staple food. This one at a time of supply constraints.
The two sources, a Singapore-based trader, told Reuters that importing countries, including Indonesia and the Philippines, had to pay between $ 30 and $ 80 per tonne more than the agreed price in the agreement. The price was about $ 550 a tonne for Vietnamese fragrant rice before India banned white rice exports in July.
The price increase gives rice sellers an additional income of about $ 15 million to $ 40 million, compared to the price agreed before the export ban from India.
"Buyers have agreed to pay a higher price for some of the rice they bought for distribution in August," a trader from an international trading company told Reuters. He added that about 200,000 tonnes of rice had been distributed this month, while another 300,000 tonnes had not yet been unloaded at Vietnamese ports.
World rice prices have risen by about 20 per cent, the highest level in 15 years since India, which accounts for about 40 per cent of global supply, has banned the export of non-basmati white rice. (basmati) last month. India's decision has cut rice in the world market by 10 million tonnes, or 20 percent.
