World-class clothing company H&M investigates garment workers' rights violations in Myanmar
H&M has told Reuters it is pursuing 20 allegations of labor abuse at a garment factory in Myanmar that supplies garments to the world's second-largest retailer. This one.
The UK-based human rights advocacy group monitored 156 cases of abuse of workers at Myanmar's garment factories from February 2022 to February 2023, up from 56 last year. Decline in workers' rights since the military coup in February 2021.
Reuters reports, citing a report by the NGO Center for Business and Human Rights (BHRRC) that wage cuts and wage theft were the most frequently reported allegations since then. Unfair dismissals, inhumane employment rates and forced overtime.
"All cases raised in the BHRRC report are being monitored and, if necessary, resolved through our local team on a case-by-case basis," H&M said in a statement. "Cooperate closely with stakeholders."
The Swedish retailer said: "We are deeply concerned about the latest developments in Myanmar and we are seeing an increase in the challenges of our operations in line with our standards. "Our needs."
