Sri Lanka lifts ban on imports of trucks and lorries
Sri Lanka on Tuesday lifted the ban on imports of trucks and other heavy vehicles for the first time since March 2020. According to Reuters.
Sri Lanka has been gradually easing import restrictions imposed as the country's economy plunged into a financial crisis caused by a severe dollar shortage.
However, this is the first time that the South Asian country has lifted some restrictions on vehicles imposed in March 2020.
However, the ban on the import of passenger cars, including minivans, will remain in effect.
Sri Lanka's economy has stabilized in the last nine months after receiving a $ 2.9 billion bailout from the International Monetary Fund (IMF), bringing inflation back under control. And help the country's international currency reserves recover.
Sri Lanka's foreign exchange reserves rose 26 percent to a 19-month high of $ 3.8 billion as of July, driven by stronger remittances and tourism revenue. Sri Lanka's currency has appreciated by about 13.5 percent this year, according to data from the Central Bank of Sri Lanka. According to Reuters.
