Japan brings seven state-of-the-art technology companies to explore and help develop Cambodia's agricultural sector
(Phnom Penh): The Japan External Trade Organization (JETRO) and the Japan Aerospace Exploration Agency (JAXA) have brought together seven Japanese state-of-the-art technology companies to jointly study, develop and improve agriculture in Cambodia through the latest Japanese technology.
At the conference on the establishment of joint cooperation between Cambodia and Japan, the Japan External Trade Organization and the Japan Space Exploration Agency introduced seven Japanese state-of-the-art technology companies to jointly improve the agricultural sector in Cambodia.
"I am confident that these innovative businesses will be a driving force in improving the efficiency and profitability of Cambodia's agricultural sector," the ambassador said.
Mr. Oknha Sok Piseth stated that Cambodia and Japan have a close and long-standing relationship. Japan is a foreign investor and one of Cambodia's largest trading partners. In fact, in 2022, bilateral trade between the two countries is about $ 2.4 billion, while Japanese investment in Cambodia is about $ 2.9 billion, according to data from the Council for the Development of Cambodia (CDC).
He said that some of Japan's major investments in Cambodia include supermarkets, car assembly plants, motors, electrical equipment or equipment factories and hospitals. In terms of agricultural cooperation between the two countries, dating back to the 1990s, Japan has provided technical assistance and equipment to support the rehabilitation of irrigation and rice production in Cambodia.
In recent years, Japan has introduced Cambodia to monitor emissions and innovations using satellite technology in order to enhance the resilience and sustainability of the agricultural sector.
"Through the use of such technology, Cambodia will be able to enhance its agricultural competitiveness, reduce greenhouse gas emissions and promote a culture of innovation among the local population," he said.
"I think this is the best time to introduce high-tech companies to Cambodia. In fact, we have seven high-tech companies and associations that provide solutions to improve the agricultural sector in Cambodia," she said.
