He explained that the issuance of joint venture units must be done through a fund management company licensed by the Securities and Exchange Commission of Cambodia. As for projects that require the public to participate in the investment, they must be registered with the same regulator of the Securities and Exchange Commission of Cambodia before they can be issued for sale to the public. That is, a package of funds that is taken in small portions, which can cost from $ 1 or more.
"For example, investing in a project requires a package of $ 10 million," he added. "If we take $ 10 million and divide it by $ 1 million, there may be no buyers, but if we take that $ 10 million and turn it into small units worth only $ 1, there may be a lot of people interested in investing."
According to Sou Socheat, the Securities and Exchange Commission of Cambodia has so far licensed 10 companies and two companies have applied for investment projects.
According to the Prakas on Licensing and Management of Joint Venture Businesses, a number of key players with different roles and responsibilities are required to ensure safety and transparency in the overall venture business process.
The fund management company then uses the funds raised to invest in one or more investment projects that it deems profitable and distributes those profits to investors in accordance with the principles agreed between the fund management company and the fund. Investors.
