Public debt in Pacific countries expected to fall (Video inside) (Wellington): The World Bank says most public debt in Pacific countries is expected to fall in 12 months. As countries move toward a gradual easing of stimulus measures after the Kovid-19 crisis, the fiscal situation will improve.
"In line with fiscal consolidation efforts, public debt is projected to decline during 2023-2024," Reuters reported, citing a World Bank report on the Pacific economy. "Throughout the Pacific (except in Solomon Islands and the federal state of Micronesia)."
The World Bank has said that six countries in the Pacific, namely Kiribati, the Republic of the Marshall Islands, the Federated States of Micronesia, Samoa, Tonga and Tuvalu, are at high risk of a debt crisis.
However, the report added that as the fiscal deficit widens in Solomon Islands and the federal state of Micronesia, these governments are expected to increase lending to meet the financing shortfall, which is raising public debt. According to Reuters.
