The Beijing-born man graduated from high school in September 1969, when he was sent to northeast China for training at a military engineering company in northeastern Hai. Long Jiang, which lasted up to eight years.
In September 1989, Wang was sent by the Chinese government to work at the Chinese Embassy in Tokyo, Japan for a five-year term, and returned to China in March 1994. After visiting and studying abroad, including in the United States, from 1994 to 1999, Wang was appointed Chinese Deputy Foreign Minister in February 2001.
In September 2004, he was appointed Chinese Ambassador to Tokyo, Japan, holding office until September 2007. In June 2008, Wang Yi replaced Jin Yunlin as director of the Taiwan Affairs Bureau under the State Council of the People's Republic of China.
Until March 16, 2013, after the inauguration of Chinese President Xi Jinping, Wang was officially appointed Chinese Foreign Minister with the support of the Chinese People's Congress. In 2018, he was promoted to Chinese State Councilor. He is also a member of the Political Bureau and chairman of the Foreign Affairs Committee of the CPC Central Committee.
