Typhoon Khanun weakened to a tropical storm as it made landfall in North Korea overnight Thursday, and military and ruling party officials were deployed to respond to the impact. North Korean local newspapers reported that officials were ordered to implement a disaster response system at all times and make evacuation plans.
In some parts of South Korea, rainfall has risen to 400 mm since Thursday with a maximum wind speed of 126 km per hour, flooding villages, schools and roads. Nearly 16,000 people were evacuated, but about 60 percent of them returned home Thursday morning (local time) as about 350 flights and 450 railways were canceled.
With the lack of existing infrastructure and deforestation factors exacerbating flood risks, North Korea is also struggling with hurricanes that could cause further damage and disrupt crop recovery plans.
