Singapore set September 1 for presidential election if more than one candidate is nominated (Video inside) (Singapore): The Singapore government announced on Friday that Singapore will hold its first presidential election on Friday. Next September, if more than one candidate is nominated for this position. This was released by the AFP news agency on Friday, August 11, 2023.
However, if only one candidate is nominated on August 22, that person will be declared the new president. The current president of Singapore, Halimah Yacob, the city's first female president, has said she has decided not to run again as her term expires on September 13. Haley Mayakob took office in 2017.
Singapore has a population of about 3.5 million, of which about three-quarters are of Chinese descent, 12.5 per cent are of Malaysian descent and 9 per cent are of Indian descent. The rest are classified as of Eurasian origin. Although Singapore has a president, the executive power is under the control of the prime minister. The Prime Minister of Singapore is Lee Hsien Loong, who has been in power since 2004.
