The research team proposes to help expand the use of electronic devices in hospitals to provide a wider range of HIV services across the country.
(Phnom Penh): The research team requested the National Center for AIDS, Skin and STIs as well as the Ministry of Health to expand and promote the use of electronic devices in hospitals, clinics that provide other HIV treatment services in Across Cambodia.
Professor Saphon Vattanak stated that the request was made after the research team found that the use of tablets as a technological tool for facilitating communication between patients and providers and training on how to communicate between patients. And providers can really help improve the use of HIV treatment services.
"As a result of the research, most HIV / AIDS providers support the use of communication technology in the health sector and find that remote communication through technology greatly facilitates communication with patients," he said. Especially during the Kovid-19.
At the same time, the HIV service providers involved in this project still maintain the use of communication technology with some patients, who sometimes find it difficult to travel to receive treatment directly at hospitals, clinics, and HIV services. Although our study project is over.
Most of the health officials who attended the training on building trust and good communication between patients and health care providers with community engagement experts felt that this training was important and provided experience and Good lessons for them in preparing words and behaviors to make communication with patients more effective and compassionate.
In addition, the number of patients missing from quarterly medical services decreased in all five HIV / AIDS hospitals / clinics after the study project provided Tablets to facilitate long-distance communication between patients and providers. Training on increasing trust and good communication between patients and health care providers to the staff - staff of those clinics.
The Secretary of State said that the evidence obtained from this research is that patients missing from the quarterly treatment services have decreased to the five hospitals / clinics of HIV treatment services after implementing the mechanism of communication through technology with you. The disease.
Annual monsoon rains cause landslides and floods claim 38 lives in Nepal
Landslides caused by heavy rains have killed one person and cut off a highway in Nepal this week as the annual monsoon death toll rises to a record high, officials said on Tuesday. Nearly 40 people. This is according to the release by the AFP news agency on Tuesday, August 8, 2023.
The rains that fall on Nepal from June to September kill dozens of people every year and many more are missing as monsoons cause landslides and floods. An Interior Ministry official said 38 people had been killed this year and at least 33 were still missing.
Rescuers are searching for six people missing after a landslide brought their home to a village near the capital Kathmandu. A major highway connecting the capital with the southern plains has also been temporarily closed due to landslides.
