Meteorologists in Sweden and Norway have issued a red alert, the worst level for several days this week, and said the worst-hit areas received the same amount of rain. Usually a month or more in just 24 hours.
Three people were injured and taken to hospital after a train carrying more than 100 passengers overturned in eastern Sweden after heavy rains battered parts of the tracks, police said.
Strong winds and thunderstorms cut off electricity in the region and disrupted ferries in the Baltic Sea, as well as some air traffic, while Norway suspended some train services and delayed some football matches.
The low-pressure system that hit Sweden on Sunday night and arrived in Norway on Monday was named Hans.
Authorities have warned the most vulnerable to stay away from rivers and slopes and not to travel unless absolutely necessary. They said the risk of property damage was high as rain was expected to continue in the coming days.
Denmark, a neighboring country, also saw heavy rains and issued a yellow alert, a low-level warning, while meteorologists in Finland said the country could also experience thunderstorms. Strong this weekend as well.
