BEIJING, Aug. 7 (Xinhua) - International weekend talks in Saudi Arabia on a peaceful solution to the Ukraine crisis have helped "unite international opinion," the Chinese Foreign Ministry said on Monday. This is according to the release by the AFP news agency on Monday, August 7, 2023.
More than 40 countries, including China, India, the United States and the European Union, except Russia, joined the Jeddah talks that ended Sunday.
China sent its special envoy for Eurasia, Li Hui, a former Chinese ambassador to Russia, to visit six European capitals in May to find common ground for a political solution to the now-defunct Ukraine war. It has reached its 18th month.
In a written statement to Reuters, the Foreign Ministry said Li had "extensive relations and communication with all parties on the political solution to the Ukraine crisis ... listened to the views and suggestions of all parties. "And push for more international consensus."
The statement added that all parties spoke positively of Li Hui's participation in the meeting and fully supported China's positive role in promoting peace talks.
Analysts say China's presence indicates a possible shift in Beijing's approach, but not a shift in support for Moscow.
Beijing has refused to condemn Moscow's February 2022 invasion. But it has proposed its peace plan, which has been warmly welcomed in Russia and Ukraine, while the United States and NATO are skeptical. The two-day meeting in Jeddah is part of Ukraine's diplomatic efforts to gain support outside the West.
