Oknha Song Saran, Chairman of the Board of the Cambodian Rice Federation, told Fresh News that after India shut down rice exports, the price of rice has risen to an all-time high for more than 10 years. 20%, which makes farmers enjoy this increase in rice prices. Currently, the price of rice has increased slightly, from 4 to 5%.
He added that the case of higher rice prices than rice prices will cause Cambodia's rice exports to decline.
He added that this is an important time for Cambodia to manage data on rice exports to Vietnam, which could boost from informal exports to formal exports. At the same time, it is necessary to strengthen the export volume control to some extent so that local mills can buy stocks for food storage in the next one or two months until the rainy season rice harvest during November. Ahead.
"We conclude that the world does not have some food, the amount that each country has imported in the last six to seven months is almost 20 million tons of white rice, which is enough for each country, but "This panic purchase is just a matter of climate change and food security, and the country is rushing to buy rice to store, not to eat now, but to stockpile."
Kao Thach, CEO of ARDB Bank, said that the increase in rice prices will bring good results for farmers who produce, which makes farmers more profitable. But exporters may be affected by the high price of rice, making it impossible for them to buy and process it for export.
At the same time, he worries that white rice, a type of rice exported to Vietnam, may increase in the domestic market. However, this is only a preliminary conclusion as the harvest in some provinces has just begun.
According to the report of the Cambodian Rice Federation, in the first seven months of 203, Cambodia exported 362,708 tons of rice, earning more than $ 253 million. In particular, 2,498,586 tons of rice was exported, valued at 663.51 million US dollars, of which 60% was exported through the request for certification of export documents from the competent authorities.
