India, the world's largest exporter of rice, recently banned the export of white and broken rice in an effort to maintain food security in its country, pushing up prices. White rice on the market in some countries in the region seems to have increased significantly.
Following India's announcement, it was seen as an opportunity for Cambodia's neighbors Thailand and Vietnam to increase exports at a higher price. Cambodia, on the other hand, may benefit farmers from rising rice prices, especially plain rice, not fragrant rice.
However, Duke Kim Savuth sees it as a risk for Cambodia. He said that because now the price of ordinary rice has risen sharply because Vietnam buys too high, especially in the provinces bordering Vietnam. This increase in the price of regular rice has also pushed up the price of rice in the market.
According to the well-known rice trader, in Takeo province, Vietnamese traders came to buy at 1,250 riel per kilogram for regular rice.
On this issue, Dr. Kao Thach, the Royal Government Delegate in charge of the Director General of the Rural Development Bank for Agriculture and Rural Development (ARDB), held a meeting with the Cambodian Rice Federation, millers and rice exporters to discuss the situation of the rice sector. Rice, rice exports and challenges at the beginning of this year's harvest.
At the same time, Dr. Kao Thach expressed concern over the current rise in rice prices, which has led to higher rice prices and lower exports. He said ARDB would continue to monitor the situation.
