According to legend, Preah Ang Chek Preah Ang Cham was a goddess, the former daughter of Suryavarman II, the founder of Angkor Wat.
The reason why the people have faith, interest and worship of Princess Banana King is because of her honesty, honesty, gentleness and high compassion, which both of them always sympathize to give alms to all the people who The two princesses needed special help, and they educated the prisoners to follow the teachings of Buddhism and set them free.
The two princesses were devout Buddhists, and the people of the kingdom were aware of their wonderful personality from generation to generation until the reign of King Ang Chan Reachea. In the 16th century, he allowed the construction of a banana statue for the general public to worship.
After these two statues made the people live in prosperity, the people considered them as sacred and famous Buddha statues all over the country. Not only that, both statues became attractive for rich people. Powerful and corrupt merchants as they begin to plot to steal and sell property.
That is why the image of Banana King Cham disappeared for a while during the Angkor area was abandoned. However, both statues were rediscovered in 1950 in the jungles of Angkor Thom by Siem Reap Angkor Conservation Officials, who are believed to have preserved the banana tree. In the temples of Angkor Wat.
When the Khmer Rouge came to power in 1975-1979, the banana statue of King Cham was dropped by Khmer Rouge soldiers into the Siem Reap River and buried in the mud at the bottom of the river until Siem Reap was liberated in 1979
It is believed that the Khmer Rouge soldiers who were carrying the two statues to the Siem Reap River died at the hands of the King of Banana shortly after the two Buddha statues were dropped. In the river water.
Later, in 1982, Preah Chao Athikar Wat Damnak, with the participation of the Governor of Siem Reap, Neou Sam, built a small hermitage near the King's Palace in the Independence Park or King's Park and celebrated the procession of Buddha statues. Two from Wat Damnak came and kept there.
Today, it has become a place for Buddhists and Cambodians all over the country to worship and pray for peace according to their individual desires through their respective prayers.
After that, Samdech Kittisanghabandit also visited some temples in Siem Reap.
