The "Zero Trap" campaign has raised awareness among more than 14 million people as the Ministry of Environment, local authorities and partner organizations are committed to continuing the campaign to end the trap crisis in Cambodia.
(Phnom Penh): For 13 months, between March 2022 and June 2023, the Ministry of Environment, Sub-National Administration, local authorities and partner organizations have jointly implemented zero trapping campaigns in urban areas and districts in some provinces. 12: Stung Treng, Preah Vihear, Kratie, Mondulkiri, Kampong Thom, Ratanakiri, Pursat, Veal Veng, Kampong Speu, Koh Kong, Siem Reap, Battambang and Pailin.
The Zero Trap campaign, which was initiated and led by the Ministry of Environment and with the support and cooperation of partner organizations, ended with the proudest success. The main goal of this campaign is to end the trap crisis and the illegal wildlife trade in Cambodia.
This was emphasized by Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment and Chairman of the Zero Trap Campaign in the meeting to review the results of the Zero Trap Campaign held on August 3, 2023 at the Ministry of Environment. Attended by leaders of the Ministry of Environment, members of the National Assembly, leaders and representatives of sub-national administrations, local authorities and partner organizations in the field of environment.
He recommended: "I would like to encourage the Ministry of Environment, Sub-National Administration and all partner organizations to continue to implement this campaign in the provinces where it has been implemented and to start in other new geographical locations." In addition, to make the movement of this campaign more vibrant and strong and for Cambodia to be able to eliminate traps from protected areas to zero.
Mr. Khuth Chandara emphasized: "Strengthening the implementation of laws related to the environment, natural resources, forests, especially the Environment and Natural Resources Code, which has just been approved by the National Assembly, needs to go hand in hand with outreach campaigns to promote regional management. Protect nature and conserve wildlife more effectively.
In fact, by 2022, a total of more than 34,000 traps have been removed from protected areas in Cambodia. This figure shows a decrease in the trap rate in the forest by about 44% compared to the results of removing traps in 2021, the total number of traps collected to 61,611 traps. Meanwhile, 14,325 traps were removed from the protected area in the first half of 2023.
Based on the results of the current Trap Zero campaign, he urged all parties, especially partner organizations, to continue to cooperate with the Ministry of Environment and local authorities to improve the management of protected areas and the conservation of Cambodia's precious wildlife resources. Even better.
The report points out that the giant coyotes found in Cambodia, as well as the yellow-cheeked vultures, are among the most endangered species in the region due to the trap and wildlife crisis. Meanwhile, a cluster study by the Ministry of Environment and WWF Cambodia showed that between 2010 and 2022, the number of raccoons decreased by 89%, herds decreased by 65% and wild boar decreased by 15%.
Leaders of the Ministry of Environment, the National Assembly, sub-national administrations and partner organizations have expressed satisfaction with the results of the past zero trapping campaign and highlighted the need for the Ministry of Environment, partners and all stakeholders. Collaborate to continue this campaign with the aim of eliminating traps from natural areas and stopping the illegal wildlife trade in Cambodia.
As a follow-up recommendation, the Secretary of State encouraged all stakeholders and partners to continue to adhere to this spirit of common-will partnership in order to plan for the implementation of the Zero Trap Campaign, focusing on a number of key elements. Three are:
Second, increase the organization of more specific educational activities for consumers, sellers, buyers and wildlife traders in order to change their behavior to stop acts that society does not value at all and turn into illegal activities. They are involved in protecting wildlife, conserving biodiversity for the benefit of future generations, and contributing to the prevention of risks to public health.
And 3: more effective conservation plans should be prepared and strengthened to research and monitor the implementation of laws both inside and outside protected areas and to restore wildlife populations by encouraging local people to participate in activities. This is in order to avoid the deployment of new traps and poaching.
As a joint recommendation, the stakeholders also proposed to focus on the establishment of a joint mobile law enforcement unit at the provincial level to be responsible for combating wildlife crime outside protected areas and the adoption of preventive health measures recommended by The World Health Organization (WHO) in April 2021 on the prevention of public health risks posed by the trafficking of wildlife meat, the sale of live wildlife for food and the consumption of wild meat.
