The Taiwan Meteorological Bureau said Khanun, the second-strongest typhoon, was moving slowly toward the northeast coast with a maximum wind speed of 198 kilometers per hour. As of 9:15 a.m. (Taipei time), the typhoon, 360 kilometers from Taipei in the East China Sea, was heading west at about 5 kilometers per hour.
Northern cities, including the capital Taipei, closed businesses and schools. Taiwan's stock and foreign exchange markets were also temporarily closed. Nearly 40 international flights were canceled while domestic ferries were suspended. The Taipei subway has been reduced as hundreds of troops are deployed to respond to the disaster.
Typhoon Khanun came just a week after Typhoon Doksuri brought heavy rain and strong winds to southern Taiwan. More rain is forecast to make landfall on the northern coast of Taiwan on Thursday evening before reaching the northeast on Friday, bringing 0.6 mm of rain to the region. Central mountain and 0.3 mm mountain range near Taipei.
