Thai, Vietnamese rice exporters renegotiate contracts amid rising rice prices (Video inside) (Singapore): Thai and Vietnamese rice exporters are renegotiating contract prices of around half a million. Tons for export in August due to India's export ban tightening global supplies. This is according to a source from the rice trading sector told Reuters.
Rice exporters are rushing to find supplies from farmers who have raised prices after rising prices in world markets put multi-million dollar price deals at risk.
India, the world's largest rice exporter, late last month banned the export of non-basmati rice amid uncertainty over domestic production, raising supply concerns. Food Among the major rice importers in Asia and Africa.
"Prices have risen since India banned exports and it has been difficult for suppliers to meet signed contracts," a Singapore-based trader who witnessed the talks told Reuters. At a low price.
Thailand and Vietnam, the world's second and third largest rice exporters, are expected to export more than one million tonnes in August. India is the world's largest exporter of rice, accounting for about 40% of the world's supply.
Both sources said the global price of rice for major rice varieties exported worldwide has risen by about $ 80 per tonne since India imposed a ban on July 20.
