Under his leadership, he strengthened governance through fruitful reforms, thus giving new impetus to the fundamental principles of democracy, the rule of law, justice, social and economic development, and equal opportunities for the people. Cambodia and the world. Most notably, he showed that peace can only be achieved through open, honest and constructive negotiations.
This unwavering initiative has brought peace to Cambodia, now building trusting relations with the international community. As a result, Cambodia is not only known as the "Island of Peace in the Mekong Region" but also "a small country with a big heart."
Cambodia is the only Buddhist country that states that Buddhism is the state religion. However, compared to other countries in the Mekong region, religious freedom in Cambodia is a role model to learn from. As a result, all religions respect one another and communicate in harmony and peace.
Due to the common values of Buddhism, Prime Minister Hun Sen met with King Norodom Sihanouk during his exile in the 1980s.
Negotiations to end the civil war and achieve national unity for the country began then. As a result, the Paris Peace Accords were signed on October 23, 1991, thanks to the efforts of both of them and the international community. This process was followed by the establishment of the United Nations Transitional Authority in Cambodia (UNTAC) to administer the first general election in 1993, leading to the formation of a coalition government.
After the term of UNTAC ended in 1993, the peace and reconciliation process resumed. At that time, the remaining Khmer Rouge boycotted the 1993 election and rejected the request for integration into the national army. But in 1996, Prime Minister Hun Sen embarked on a popular policy: win-win politics.
This effective end-of-war strategy did not hurt anyone. It ensured the security and protection of all Khmer Rouge forces and their families and integrated them into a single government system. This is the virtue of win-win politics, built on the moral values of the Middle Way and the compassion of Buddhism.
Cambodia achieved peace at the end of 1998, thanks to its Buddhist values and people-centered development. Cambodia ended decades of civil war and paved the way for greater peace in the Mekong region and ASEAN. Without peace in Cambodia, there would be no peace in the Mekong region and ASEAN. Through this suffix, Cambodia shares the dividends of its peace with the region and the world.
Under Cambodia's judiciary, senior Khmer Rouge leaders were tried. This process has allowed the nation to receive Buddhist spiritual healing, while also receiving justice under national and international law. The ECCC completed its work and closed in December 2022.
Through this judicial mechanism, the people have achieved peace and justice, and there is no longer any retaliation against each other, which is the basis for ensuring the long-term national development and the environment of Cambodia's full integration with the world. Moreover, the success of the ECCC has become an important foundation of education for the younger generation and a model for world peace-building.
In 2022, Prime Minister Hun Sen launched the "Two States, One Nation: One Peninsula, One People, One Culture" initiative, based on the values of Buddhism for peaceful reunification on the Korean Peninsula.
Overall, Buddhist democracy in Cambodia has had a profound impact on social, cultural, governance, and public affairs, including political, economic, and foreign policy. This Buddhist democracy is a solid foundation for the development of Cambodia to achieve peace, justice, harmony and prosperity for the people of Cambodia and the people of other countries in the world.
