It should also be noted that the Cambodian Sustainable Landscape and Ecotourism Sustainability Project (CSLEP) is a project of the Royal Government of Cambodia, which has been led by the Ministry of Environment and the Ministry of Rural Development under the World Bank funding and aims to improve Manage protected areas and promote opportunities for ecotourism development to increase the value chain of non-timber forest products in the Cardamom and Tonle Sap landscape.
Currently, the project has been supporting 30 natural communities in seven target provinces, including Koh Kong, Kampong Speu, Kampong Chhnang, Pursat, Battambang, Kampong Thom and Siem Reap. Cranes census on the rise as the first scientific record of crab nests in the Mekong Delta over the last 20 years
(Phnom Penh): The crane is the heaviest and tallest bird and can fly long distances, which is currently threatened with extinction due to declining population. According to the census data, the highest recorded numbers in Cambodia and Vietnam were 878 in 2002, down to 156 in 2022.
The dates for the crane case in Cambodia are December, January, February, March, April and May. The latest census results show the highest number of cranes in February was 180. The new figure shows a positive sign compared to the 2022 census of 156 and the 2021 census of 161. Two cranes were recorded in Vietnam during the census.
Excited, Eam Sam Onn, director of WWF Wildlife Research, highlighted the significance of the findings, stating that it was the first scientific record of a nesting nest with a reproductive adult. Also in the Mekong River flooded forest for the past 20 years.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra praised the research efforts of the scientists of the Ministry of Environment and WWF and encouraged the team to continue researching, protecting and managing this crane sanctuary and all wildlife in the sanctuary. Better to conserve wildlife and natural resources for the sake of conservation and long-term local community economic development.
The new findings bring hope for the conservation of birds in protected areas and other natural habitats throughout Cambodia. It should also be noted that in 2021, Cambodia recorded a total of 156 cranes within the framework of this census in major sanctuary areas throughout the country.
"Cambodia still has a significant number of cranes in its natural habitat, but it is facing many threats that we must address together immediately," said Bou Rossak, director of Life in Cambodia.
Taking this opportunity, he would like to thank the Minister of Environment for establishing the Cambodian Crane Conservation Working Group to accumulate resources to implement the 10-year crane conservation plan (2020-2030) in Cambodia.
