Russian Defense Ministry: Three Ukrainian drones were shot down from the air in Moscow.
MOSCOW (Reuters) - Three Ukrainian drones were shot down in Moscow on Sunday morning in an attack that destroyed several office buildings, the Russian Defense Ministry said on Sunday. Two and temporarily closed an international airport. This is according to the release by the AFP news agency on Sunday, July 30, 2023.
One drone was shot down on the outskirts of the city, while two others were intercepted by military satellites and crashed into an office building, but no one was injured.
Moscow and its environs, about 500 km from the Ukrainian border, were rarely targeted during the Ukraine conflict, but this year have been hit by drones several times.
The state-run TASS news agency reported that Vnukovo airport was closed and flights were diverted to other airports. But in less than an hour, operations seem to be back to normal.
Earlier this month, drone strikes also disrupted air traffic at the airport southwest of the capital.
On Friday, the Russian Defense Ministry also announced that it had thwarted a Ukrainian drone strike on Moscow. Attacks on Moscow have taken place in recent weeks as Ukraine launches a counterattack operation to retake Russian territory.
