About three days before the exercise, the Taiwanese Defense Ministry said that mainland Chinese forces had sent dozens of fighter jets, including fighter jets and bombers, to Taiwan.
Taiwan's Defense Ministry says this week's Han Kuang exercise is designed to try to coordinate branch-to-branch cooperation and to test emergency response capabilities in the event of an aggression by the Chinese Ministry of Defense. Mainland Chinese forces enter Taiwan.
Taiwan's Han Kuang exercise on July 26 involved military helicopters flying across the air and troops deployed at Taoyuan Airport, which is considered an exercise in how to prepare various scenarios. In the face of mainland Chinese threats, this concern has grown since the outbreak of war on Ukrainian soil.
Taiwanese officials say Russia's experience in the Ukrainian war was one of the first to gain control of Ukrainian airspace, and that the experience required Taiwan to be vigilant in preventing any of its airports from falling under enemy control. No.
The 30-minute exercise at Taoyuan Airport on Wednesday saw the participation of soldiers wearing red helmets, identified as the invaders, and they carried out a series of shootings. And the police guarding the airport. Firefighters were seen carrying out their firefighting drills in preparation for a Chinese offensive.
Military helicopters were seen flying over the airport with enemy troops deployed on the airport grounds. As enemy troops approached the building near the airport, enemy soldiers opened fire on the streets with Taiwanese troops stationed in the area and clashed with other soldiers hiding in the hideout. Temporary.
The 30-minute exercise ended as Taiwanese troops carried out the clearing of the remaining enemy forces by hoisting the Taiwanese flag at the end to demonstrate their latest successful act of successfully defending the airport. During the exercise, additional air control measures were put in place, temporarily banning aircraft from landing or taking off.
