The NEC Trial Chamber decided to remove the names of five individuals from the voter list for 5 to 10 years and a fine of 10 to 15 million riels each in connection with incitement to destroy the ballots.
Mr. Prach Chan, President of the NEC, presided over the Trial Chamber, read out the decision in Case 001 with plaintiff Ly Meng Haing in Tbong Khmum province, upholding and fining 10 million Riel and deleting his name. From the voter list and disenfranchised to stand for election for 10 years, regardless of other criminal offenses.
For Case 002, there is a male plaintiff named Khem Monikosal in Pailin Province. The NEC Trial Chamber fined 15 million Riels and removed his name from the voter list and disqualified him from standing for 10 years. Years, regardless of other criminal penalties.
For Case 003, there is a female plaintiff named Sun Sophea in Pailin Province. The NEC Trial Chamber upheld and fined 10 million Riels and removed the name from the voter list and disqualified him from standing for election. 10 years, regardless of other criminal penalties.
The President of the NEC stated that this decision will be heard and announced in public on 27 July 2023 in the presence of the plaintiffs at the NEC and the lawyers representing the defendants at the NEC. Protest to the Constitutional Council in accordance with the law.
Hang Puthea, a member and spokesman for the NEC, said: "Of course, a commission of inquiry has been set up and it has been found that the Trial Chamber's decision The NEC also reviewed the facts and the law, the NEC decided to uphold the decision of the Trial Chamber of the Capital-Provincial Election Commission.
If Mr. Hang Puthea emphasized that the decision to uphold the decision of the Trial Chamber of the Capital-Provincial Election Commission means to keep the fine of 10 to 15 million Riels to be removed from the voter list and Withdraw the right to stand for election as provided by law.
Please be informed that according to the new Article 142 of the Law on the Amendment of the Election Law, which came into force on July 4, 2023, inciting, harassing or preventing people from going to the polls or destroying ballots is an act. Illegal. This offense is fined from 5 million to 20 million riels, regardless of other criminal penalties.
