The data show that there is an aging Japanese population across the country and that foreigners are playing an increasingly important role in creating a declining population. As of January 1, 2023, according to the Housing Registration Data released by the Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications of Japan, the population has declined for the 14th year to 122.42 million. This is the first time there has been a decline in population across Japan's 47 prefectures.
The number of foreigners living in Japan was 2.99 million, an increase of 10.7% compared to 2022, the highest year-on-year increase since the Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications of Japan began recording data. Ten years ago.
Japan's total population has dropped by about 511,000. As of January 1, 2020, before Kovid 19 spread around the world, there were 2.87 million foreigners living in Japan.
