Letter to readers: A normal moment of Khmer-style pacifist democracy!
(Phnom Penh): For foreign observers, they are surprised that the election in Cambodia starts very early at 7 am. But for Cambodians, this is just a normal day for us. We like to go early in the morning to avoid queuing in the hot sun.
On July 23, at the polling station at the new Samdech Hun Sen High School in Phnom Penh, there was a traffic jam around the school entrance between 7 am and 8 am due to voters wanting to go to the polls early. Inside the campus, the atmosphere is lively. People were smiling and taking pictures on their mobile phones in front of the polling station, showing their ink-stained fingerprints.
Voters, observers and election organizers all behave in a calm and orderly manner, as if they are already accustomed to this process. By 1:00 pm, polling stations were becoming less crowded as more than 70% of eligible voters had already gone to the polls before the polls closed at 3:00 pm.
Voter turnout rose to 84.58%, higher than the last four national elections since 2003. We have never heard of any violence. We do not see people with eyes full of anger, hatred and discrimination compared to the 2013 and 2018 elections.
At that time, during the election campaign, when the CPP and opposition party processions met one by one, we observed that they seemed to feel full of anger and hatred on both sides. For this year, we observe the eyes of the people seem cool and soft.
For voters, their ballot represents the hope for a better future, a future that will lead to a more prosperous country by 2050 with a better quality of life for citizens and more environmentally friendly development. For business people, they are very relieved to know that Cambodia's stability remains strong. For business people, instability and unpredictability are the worst enemies of doing business.
After the last three elections, 2013, 2018, and 2023, the evolution of events can tell us that Cambodia cannot accept a Western democracy with individualism and selfishness and interests. Communities tend to weigh less than individual interests and rights.
Cambodia has our own way of adapting Western-style democratic concepts to the context of Cambodian society. This is no different from the fact that the Khmer Empire mixed Indian culture to build its unique Khmer civilization. The Khmer have established their own civilization and no foreigner thinks of the Khmer as the Indians.
Likewise, democracy in Cambodia does not need to be replicated like American and European democracies. We do not need to change our identity to please anyone. Cambodia should continue to build democracy based on our own path of peace and tolerance in the spirit of Cambodia and the teachings of Buddhism.
Cambodia really does not understand that accusing someone of being a murderer is a right to freedom of expression. Cambodia can hardly understand that after the annual shooting deaths of children in the United States, the right to carry a gun is still considered a human right. These are not Khmer attitudes and values. This is not the noble society we are trying to build. We have our Khmer way, that is, Khmer-style pacifist democracy.
