The first elections in 1993 were organized by the United Nations. In the first mandate, 120 seats in the National Assembly were contested by four political parties: FUNCINPEC won 58 seats, the Cambodian People's Party won 51 seats, and Son San's Buddhist Democratic Party won 10 seats. And Ieng Molly's Molika Party won one seat.
In the fifth mandate in 2013, there were 123 seats in the National Assembly, with eight parties competing. However, only two political parties won seats: the CPP with 68 seats and the CNRP with 55 seats.
For the sixth mandate in 2018, the number of seats in the National Assembly increased from two to 125 parties, with 20 parties competing. However, the 125 seats in the National Assembly went to the CPP.
(Phnom Penh): On the morning of Sunday, July 23, 2023, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, President of the Cambodian People's Party, and Samdech Kittiprittbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen, President The Cambodian Red Cross will go to the polls for the 7th National Assembly election at the polling station in Takhmao City Teacher Training College, Kandal Province.
History of Elections in Cambodia: Democratic elections in Cambodia resumed in 1993, following the October 23, 1991 Paris Peace Talks.
