Here is the video: Vietnam calls for domestic food security after India tightens its rice exports
HANOI (Reuters) - Vietnamese authorities on Friday called on the country's food association to ensure adequate domestic rice supply and food security after India tightened its rice exports. This is according to the release by the AFP news agency on July 21, 2023.
India, which accounts for 40 percent of the world's rice exports, on Thursday ordered a halt to its largest rice export to cut domestic prices.
"India's move to restrict rice exports will affect the global rice market," the Vietnamese Ministry of Industry and Trade said in a letter to the Vietnam Food Association.
The ministry has asked associations of rice exporters and processors to order their members to strictly abide by the law on keeping rice reserves at least 5 percent of exports for the past six months.
In a separate document, the ministry called on rice traders to balance domestic exports and sales to keep domestic prices stable.
Vietnam is the world's third largest rice exporter after India and Thailand. Rice exports in the first half of this year rose 21.3 percent from a year earlier to 4.24 million tons, according to government customs data.
