General knowledge: It took 115 years for the world to know exactly who made the first phone
Evidence shows that in 1850, Antonio left his native Italy to live in the United States, where he was also a fan of science fiction.
In 1871, Antonio applied for a patent for the Telephony at the U.S. Intellectual Property Office, but he owed another $ 10 to complete the "full" patent service, and he owed it first. Only "temporary" intellectual property.
Unfortunately, Antonio also fell ill when a ship's steam tank exploded as he was passing through Staten Island. Because he did not speak much English in the United States and needed money for physical therapy, he could not afford to pay $ 10 to complete his first device patent protection.
Five years later, in 1776, another person took the same cell phone he had already made and applied for a patent at the US Intellectual Property Office. That person was Alexander Graham Bell.
After learning of this, Antonio filed a lawsuit in court to protect the intellectual property of his phone maker. During the trial, Antonio sent prototype documents and prototype equipment to the Western Union Laboratory in the United States. The original documents of Antioch and all of them were also mysteriously lost.
While the litigation was still unresolved, Antonio died before his death, and Alexander became the victor in 1889 and was named the world's first inventor of the mobile phone. .
It was not until 2004 that some US lawmakers sought justice for Antonio and achieved what they set out to do. Since then, the world has known for sure that it was Antonio Messi who invented the first telephone in 1854. Mr. Eang Sophalleth, Leader of 7,000 Party Members, Parade to Close the National Assembly Election Campaign in Ou Raing Ov District
