The United Nations is calling on Syria to expand access to international aid to the opposition-held northwest, where more than 4 million refugees are facing starvation. Last week, the UN Security Council failed to reach an agreement to keep the Bab al-Hawa border crossing open, allowing aid agencies to enter from Turkey.
Syria was suspended from the coalition in 2011 amid a brutal crackdown by the Assad government against the so-called "Arab Spring" insurgency. According to the United Nations, armed conflict has killed 306,887 civilians and displaced more than 12 million Syrians, including 5.4 million who are living as refugees in neighboring countries. As of 2022.
