NEC Spokesman Meets International Election Observers from Japan
To begin with, Mr. Hang Puthea welcomed a group of international election observers from Japan to observe the upcoming Cambodian election, which will take place on Sunday, July 23, 2023.
Mr. Naoto Sakaguchi thanked the NEC spokespersons for taking the time to meet with the international election observation delegation from Japan while the NEC was busy preparing for the election. He recalled his work in Cambodia as a volunteer 31 years ago during the UNTAC era and as a two-term member of the Japanese National Assembly.
He stated that the participation of the International Election Observation Delegation from Japan at this time was not on behalf of the Japanese government, but his group came from a non-governmental organization based in Japan, and he now wants to meet. Find out and have some questions related to the 2023 National Assembly election.
Mr. Hang Puthea also informed the delegation about the history of the election in Cambodia that in 1993 was the election of the Constituent Assembly, which was organized by the Provisional Authority of the United Nations in Cambodia (UNTAC). . He continued that in 1998, Cambodia held its own elections and has held a series of elections until now.
So far, the NEC has held 16 regular and non-universal self-elections on a regular basis. He also mentioned the four main stages of the election for international observers to understand, including: reviewing the voter list and voter registration, registration of political parties and candidates, and campaigning. Voting and voting, vote counting and announcement of election results.
Mr. Hang Puthea added to the delegation that if there are any questions related to the election, you can contact the NEC spokesperson to inquire more about the election in Cambodia. He also thanked the Japanese government for its assistance to the National Election Committee in the past.
He hoped that through international election observers from Japan, including Naoto Sakaguchi, he would inform the Japanese government of the NEC's need to provide more assistance to the NEC. The name will be re-elected in 2026 because the voter registration materials used have been in use for 10 years. Environmental and Socialists Association calls on voters not to believe in propagandists not to go to the polls
