Ahead of the retaliatory attack, an attack on a bridge connecting the Crimea, which Putin built at a cost of about $ 4 billion, was damaged again on Monday, July 17, 2023.
Ukraine claimed responsibility for the second attack on a bridge connecting the Crimean peninsula to mainland Russia on Monday. The nearly 20-kilometer bridge, known as the Kerch Bridge, is the longest cross-strait bridge in Europe and is of strategic importance and a major symbol for the Moscow government.
Monday's attack on the bridge was the second, with the first taking place in October 2022, when a tanker exploded while crossing the bridge. There were more train fires on the bridge. The bridge, which first exploded, was repaired and reopened two months later. The same bridge was attacked for the second time on Monday, July 17, 2023.
In retaliation, as promised by Russian leaders, early in the morning, the Ukrainian port of Odessa was hit by Russian air strikes. CNN reported heavy explosions, several sirens and sirens sounded at 2 a.m. and saw Ukraine's air defense system fire down enemy shells.
Russia has reiterated its position that it will consider rejoining the agreement if it sees tangible results around its demands. UN Secretary-General Antonio Guterres has called Russia's decision a "disappointment" for the world's poor.
"Russia's decision today is a turning point for people in need of grain everywhere, but it will not stop our efforts to facilitate unhindered success," said Antonio Guterres. "Global markets for food and fertilizer products from Ukraine and Russia."
