On the morning of Tuesday, July 18, 2023, Samdech Techo and Samdech Kittipritthbandit organized the distribution of candles and gifts to 35 pagodas from Kandal Stung district. And some other districts in Kandal province.
The Presentation Ceremony of Vesa Candles and Gifts for Monks Preparing for the Upcoming Vesa Ceremony was organized by Duke Try Pheap, Personal Adviser to Samdech Techo Hun Sen, President of the Cambodian People's Party and Duke Kien Srey Neang at Wat Ang Prouch Tbeng commune, Kandal Stung district. The ceremony was also attended by the parents of the tycoon, government officials, the armed forces, leaders and employees of the company, as well as many close relatives and friends.
In the joyful and generous ceremony, Duke Try Pheap and Duke Kien Srey Neang offered candles, incense and flowers to Preah Ratanatray as a reverence. After that, Ta Achar also led various religious ceremonies, including chanting, Preah Ratanatray, Samathan Sil, etc., and handing out Preah Vossa candles and offerings to all 35 pagodas.
After handing over the Preah Vossa candles and offerings, the monks also prayed for the blessings of the Buddha, gave blessings to the Buddha, blessed the blessings, and blessed all the blessings to Samdech Techo and Samdech Kittipritthbandit, as well as the tycoon and the owner of the candles. Dedication of merits and blessings to the relatives who have passed away.
The offering of Preah Vossa candles, a tradition and a tradition of the Khmer Buddhists, always takes place before the arrival of the Vesa festival. This year's Lunar New Year will start on Wednesday, August 2, 2023.
Every year before the coming of the Vesak Day, Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Samdech Kittipritthbandit always prepare Vesa candles and gifts at all temples throughout the country, especially for Kandal district through Duke Try Pheap. Presented Preah Vossa candles and gifts to pagodas without fail.
Venerable But Sophoan, a deputy chief of Kandal Stung district and the chief abbot of Vihear Pit Meas pagoda, said that offering a Vesa candle was like offering a lantern to the Buddha statue, in remembrance of the Buddha, as a sacrifice to the merits of the Buddha. .
He also said that the offering of candles to Preah Vossa always has the merit of being a meritorious person, a high non-monk, for a person who is pure, has a habit of being born in the death of human beings, angels, and so on.
Venerable But Sophoan remarked that Samdech Techo and Samdech Kittipritthbandit, as well as Duke Try Pheap and Duke Kien Srey Neang, were Buddhists who were very generous in Buddhism and always paid close attention to the ceremony. Various of Buddhism.
In this morning's ceremony, in addition to offering Preah Vossa candles to a couple of pagodas, Duke Try Pheap and Duke Kien Srey Neang also generously donated some other gifts, including: four suffixes for 35 monks each 736,000 Riel suffix for monk's food per pagoda 1,600,000 Riel Wash 35 packs of rice per pagoda 100 kg Noodles, condensed milk, canned fish, pork, fish sauce, soy sauce, cooking oil, fruit juice, pure water.
In addition to donating candles and offerings to temples and monks, donations were also distributed to grandparents, grandfathers, abbots, pagoda committee, a total of 235 people, each received 100,000 Riels and 2 scarves. Well.
In addition, Duke Try Pheap and Duke Kien Srey Neang also prepared lunch for all 35 monks and grandparents who were invited to attend the ceremony. The event cost a total of about 70,000 US dollars.
The Vesa Candle and Gift Offering Ceremony was held in a happy and generous atmosphere. Grandma and Grandpa also expressed their happiness for receiving the gift that comes from the heart, which is pure generosity.
Mrs. San Sopheap expressed her satisfaction and happiness for this happy ceremony this morning. She also praised Duke Try Pheap for always thinking of his hometown and contributed to building many achievements in Tbeng commune, both in the field of Buddhism and the empire, such as roads and schools. In particular, the tycoon always helps the suffering of grandmothers, grandfathers and the people.
